compiler

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

Only `animate.enter` and `animate.leave` exist, but the compiler accepts any name after `animate.` and compiles every name other than `animate.enter` as a leave animation. A typo like this one:

```html
@if (show()) {
  <p animate.entr="fade-in">Hello</p>
}
```

compiles to `ɵɵanimateLeave("fade-in")`. Nothing happens when the paragraph is added. When it's removed, it gets the `fade-in` class, plays the fade-in animation on its way out, and stays in the DOM until that animation ends. `[animate.entr]`, `(animate.entr)` and the same names in host bindings behave the same way.

Nothing checks the name: `BindingParser` turns any `animate.` property or event into an animation binding, and static attributes starting with `animate.` are picked up later in the template pipeline. The pipeline then picks the kind with `name === 'animate.enter' ? AnimationKind.ENTER : AnimationKind.LEAVE` (`binding_specialization.ts`, `convert_animations.ts`), or `name.endsWith('enter')` for listeners (`ingest.ts`).

#71394 reports an error for any other `animate.` name.

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No hosted reproduction, but this is self-contained:

```ts
import {Component, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <button (click)="show.set(!show())">Toggle</button>
    @if (show()) {
      <p animate.entr="fade-in">Hello</p>
    }
  `,
  styles: `
    .fade-in {
      animation: fade-in 1s;
    }
    @keyframes fade-in {
      from {
        opacity: 0;
      }
    }
  `,
})
export class App {
  show = signal(false);
}

bootstrapApplication(App);
```

Click "Toggle" twice: the paragraph shows up without any animation, then fades in while it's being removed and only goes away after one second.

