### Description


`createRedirectResponse()` sets `Location` from request data without any length limit:

```ts
resHeaders.set("Location", location);
```

Route redirects copy request path segments into a relative `redirectTo`, and render redirects send the Router's final URL, query included, when it differs from the request (#31497). Both are intended, but nothing bounds the size. The header is at least as large as the request, and percent-encoding on the way makes it bigger: `new URL()` encodes `"` `'` `<` `>` and the Router's query serializer encodes characters like `/` `=` `?` `|`, so it is about 3x a raw request, and about 5x when a guard puts `state.url` in `returnUrl` and the `%` is encoded again. Both encodings are correct.

Behind nginx this affects other users. The response doesn't fit in nginx's default 4k `proxy_buffer_size`, so nginx answers 502 and counts it as a failed attempt for that backend (`max_fails=1`, `fail_timeout=10s`). With two backends and one worker, two unauthenticated GETs leave the upstream empty and every request gets 502 for about ten seconds, while both Node processes are fine. 

I think this is bit similar as unpoly-rails [GHSA-m875-3xf6-mf78](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-m875-3xf6-mf78).

A buffer sized for the app's largest redirect avoids it, but nothing tells the operator that size and it changes when someone adds a guard: for the plain `returnUrl` guard at nginx's 8 KB request line it is about 40 KB. `max_fails=0` also avoids it, by turning passive health checks off.

 The app doesn't need any redirect. With the server routes on `RenderMode.Server`, as in the SSR guide, this request gets a 302 with a 4,208 B `Location`:

```text
GET /home/(x)?q=<1,400 raw ">     1,412 B
```

The `**` route drops the unmatched `(x)` group, so the Router ends on `/home?q=...`. In the browser that is a `replaceState`; on the server #31497 turns it into a 302. Nothing in the Router needs to change.

### Minimal Reproduction


```ts
// app.routes.ts
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: "", component: Home },
  { path: "home", component: Home },
  { path: "**", component: NotFound },
];

// app.routes.server.ts
export const serverRoutes: ServerRoute[] = [
  { path: "**", renderMode: RenderMode.Server },
];
```

```bash
ng build
NG_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost PORT=4000 node dist/app/server/server.mjs &
NG_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost PORT=4001 node dist/app/server/server.mjs &
```

nginx with one worker and nothing else tuned:

```nginx
worker_processes 1;
events {}
http {
  upstream ssr {
    server 127.0.0.1:4000;
    server 127.0.0.1:4001;
  }
  server {
    listen 8080;
    location / {
      proxy_pass http://ssr;
      proxy_set_header Host localhost;
    }
  }
}
```

The request has to go out raw, `fetch` and `node:http` percent-encode the quotes first. I sent one per second for 25 s while normal traffic hit `/home`, and counted until `/home` answered 200 again:

| Setup                                                          | `/home` OK |
| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------: |
| nginx, the config above                                        |      15.2% |
| nginx, Compose service with two replicas                       |       9.5% |
| nginx image as shipped on 2 vCPUs (two workers)                |      25.2% |
| nginx, `worker_processes auto` with a shared `zone`            |       9.3% |
| OpenResty, plain `upstream`                                    |       4.8% |
| Kong 3.7, passive checks with 502 in `unhealthy.http_statuses` |       1.3% |
| nginx, one backend                                             |       100% |
| Same setups without those requests                             |       100% |

I also reproduced it on a VPS with nginx behind Cloudflare.


See https://github.com/SkyZeroZx/angular-ssr-location-lab 

